INT. MASCOT DRESSING ROOM - MINOR LEAGUE BASEBALL STADIUM - DAY

Cinderblock closet. A rack of foam heads: a bear, a hot dog, a giant baseball bat. Two benches, a mini-fridge, a fan that rotates uselessly.

A MAN IN A BEAR SUIT (30s, head under his arm, drenched in sweat) sits on a bench staring at his hands.

ARCHIBALD "ARCHIE" PELL (50s, team's mascot coordinator, polo shirt, clip-on tie) enters with a clipboard.

ARCHIE
Walter.

BEAR MAN
It's Wally.

ARCHIE
It's on your contract as Walter.

WALLY
Then it's Walter on paper and Wally in my heart.

Archie sits. The bench creaks.

ARCHIE
Sixth inning. The children's race.

WALLY
I know.

ARCHIE
The hot dog was supposed to win.

WALLY
I know.

ARCHIE
The hot dog is a nine-year-old with a sprained wrist now.

WALLY
He cut me off.

ARCHIE
He's nine.

WALLY
He cut me off and he looked at me.

ARCHIE
Walter.

WALLY
Wally.

ARCHIE
He was wearing the suit. He couldn't see anything.

WALLY
He saw me.

ARCHIE
Listen to me. I need you to apologize.

WALLY
To a boy dressed as a hot dog.

ARCHIE
To his mother. In person. By the concessions.

WALLY
She's terrifying.

ARCHIE
She's a dental hygienist.

WALLY
Same thing.

They both stand at the same time. Archie holds the clipboard to his chest; Wally holds the bear head to his. They say the next line together, in unison.

WALLY
We don't have to do this.

ARCHIE ^
We have to do this.

They stop. They look at each other.

ARCHIE (CONT'D)
That was strange.

WALLY
You first.

ARCHIE
No, you.

They both reach for the door at the same moment. Their hands collide on the knob. Neither lets go.

WALLY
Archie, I'm sorry about the hot dog.

ARCHIE
Walter, it's fine.

WALLY
Wally.

ARCHIE
Wally. It's fine.

He opens the door. A burst of crowd noise floods the room. Wally pulls the bear head back on, and with it, becomes a different man: huge, cheerful, waving.

Archie shakes his head and follows him out.
